From 13af2e105d1565ee2ebd8a8e3bc94c4b3cd7adf4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Oskari Rauta <oskari.rauta@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 29 Jul 2026 01:17:34 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] watchdog: ingenic: make the keepalive actually reset the
 counter

The ping handler wrote TCNT and nothing else. The block ignores that
while the counter is running: everywhere else in this driver, and in the
SoC reset path, the counter is stopped before its registers are touched
and started again afterwards. ingenic_watchdog_config() is exactly that
sequence and already zeroes TCNT in the middle of it.

So every keepalive was silently a no-op. The ioctl returned success,
userspace believed the dog was being fed, and the board reset on schedule
regardless - which is indistinguishable from the daemon never feeding at
all, and is why the fault looked for a long time like a userspace
problem.

Measured with WDIOC_GETTIMELEFT read immediately after a successful
keepalive: with a 50 second timeout it returned 20, then 4 one feed
interval later, instead of returning to 50 each time.

Ping through wdt_config() so the counter is stopped, zeroed and
restarted, like every other path that writes these registers.
---
 drivers/watchdog/ingenic_wdt.c | 19 ++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 18 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/watchdog/ingenic_wdt.c b/drivers/watchdog/ingenic_wdt.c
index 286b674f..9f1b9ddd 100644
--- a/drivers/watchdog/ingenic_wdt.c
+++ b/drivers/watchdog/ingenic_wdt.c
@@ -64,9 +64,26 @@ struct ingenic_wdt_drvdata {
 	struct notifier_block restart_handler;
 };
 
+static void wdt_config(struct ingenic_wdt_drvdata *drvdata,
+					   unsigned int new_timeout);
+
 static int ingenic_wdt_ping(struct watchdog_device *wdt_dev)
 {
-	ingenic_watchdog_set_count(0);
+	struct ingenic_wdt_drvdata *drvdata = watchdog_get_drvdata(wdt_dev);
+
+	/*
+	 * Writing TCNT while the counter is running does nothing - the block
+	 * ignores it. Everywhere else that touches these registers stops the
+	 * counter first, programs it, and starts it again; ingenic_watchdog_config()
+	 * is exactly that sequence and it zeroes TCNT in the middle of it.
+	 *
+	 * So a ping that only wrote TCNT was silently a no-op: the keepalive
+	 * ioctl succeeded, userspace believed the dog was being fed, and the
+	 * board reset on schedule anyway. Measured with WDIOC_GETTIMELEFT read
+	 * straight after a successful ping - it kept counting down across pings
+	 * instead of returning to the full timeout.
+	 */
+	wdt_config(drvdata, wdt_dev->timeout);
 	return 0;
 }
 
